<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>Label</key>
	<string>com.apex-win.daemon</string>

	<!--
	@BINDIR@ must be substituted with the real install prefix's bin
	directory before this file is installed (by scripts/install-macos.sh).
	launchd's ProgramArguments does not do $PATH lookup the way a shell
	would, and it must be an absolute path, so a placeholder here would
	silently fail rather than fail loudly if left unsubstituted. Homebrew
	uses /opt/homebrew/bin on Apple Silicon and /usr/local/bin on Intel; a
	single hardcoded path here would be wrong for one of the two.
	-->
	<key>ProgramArguments</key>
	<array>
		<string>@BINDIR@/win-sandbox-runner</string>
		<string>--daemon</string>
	</array>

	<!--
	RunAtLoad + KeepAlive: start once at login and restart on unexpected
	exit, the launchd analogue of the systemd unit's
	"WantedBy=multi-user.target" + "Restart=on-failure". SuccessfulExit
	false means "only restart on crash", matching systemd's on-failure (not
	on Restart=always, which would also relaunch after a clean "stop" command).
	-->
	<key>RunAtLoad</key>
	<true/>
	<key>KeepAlive</key>
	<dict>
		<key>SuccessfulExit</key>
		<false/>
	</dict>

	<!--
	This is a LaunchAgent (~/Library/LaunchAgents), not a LaunchDaemon
	(/Library/LaunchDaemons): it must run as the logged-in user, not root.

	On Linux the daemon runs as root because it registers a binfmt_misc
	handler in /proc/sys/fs/binfmt_misc, which requires root, and that
	handler is what makes bare "./game.exe" transparently re-exec through
	win-sandbox-runner. macOS has no binfmt_misc equivalent, so nothing here
	needs or should run as root; see crates/win-sandbox-runner/src/daemon.rs
	run_daemon()'s platform split on the geteuid() check for the Rust side
	of this same decision.

	Concretely, this means the double-click experience on macOS does NOT
	depend on this daemon at all: double-click routes through
	APEX-WIN.app -> Launch Services -> the exe flag, which loads rules/app-db
	fresh on every launch and dispatches directly, the same as Linux's own
	Path A (see HANDOFF.md). This daemon exists purely as a CLI convenience
	for a user who runs `win-sandbox-runner some.exe` (a positional-arg
	invocation, not the exe flag) repeatedly from Terminal and wants the app
	database and rules pre-loaded rather than re-read from disk on every
	invocation, mirroring Linux's Path B minus the actual kernel-level
	interception, which has no macOS equivalent.
	-->

	<key>StandardOutPath</key>
	<string>/tmp/win-sandbox-runner.daemon.log</string>
	<key>StandardErrorPath</key>
	<string>/tmp/win-sandbox-runner.daemon.log</string>
</dict>
</plist>
